A||||5||David Gamache||Smog problem on 88 fiero 2.5L||||1||
Z||000000||David Gamache||01-12-2000||12:22 AM||||I have an 88 fiero 2.5L motor that has become a pain this year to smog. California has strict smog laws and for no apparent reason the nox(NO)readings are high on this car. All other readings,CO2,02 are good. It has never been a problem before, but the test requirements are getting stricter and this year at 150,000 miles the car will not pass. The O2 sensor has been relpaced as well as a tune-up was done,plugs(which were burning white to coco brown), plugwires, air filter, egr and the pcv is ok. The motor runs good and does not burn any oil. Any thoughts on this would be helpful. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||01-12-2000||12:27 AM||||My V6 wont pass either. I have it running the best it has ever run since I have had it. I am at a loss. I am just not going to smog it||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-12-2000||12:28 AM||||Help me out you chemists... Isn't NOX an indicator of a failing/bad cat?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000003||862M4inCA||01-12-2000||07:03 AM||||Well I wouldn't say it always would indicate that. Back in June when I smogged mine I was passing on everything except my HCppm limits. Finally decided to replace the cat and when I pulled the old one out I basically just had the shell of a cat. All of the material inside had broke up and was shoved into my muffler. If there is a NOx problem the thing to look for is a working EGR system. Remember that NOx forms when you get extremely high combustion temps (2500+ degrees. EGR helps to keep the temps lower. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||01-12-2000||12:01 PM||||Same as 862M4inCA, my cat was empty and I just barely failed HC.  NOX was high but well within the limits even without a cat.  I would be looking at the EGR since it is  specifically designed to reduce NOX emissions.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-11-2000||01:52 PM||||When the combustion temps get to high, Nox is formed. The EGR reduces the combustion temperatures. A failed EGR will raise the Nox. <P>Too much advance will too, but unless you have the dist cranked up, I doubt that's it.<P>I would check the EGR first.<P>Are you thread-milled or tailpipe only? The EGR is closed at idle, so if you are failing idle, it won't help. But if you are thread-milled at 25 and 35, it certainly could affect it.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-11-2000).]||||reg||1
